abstract: Bao mian lan chu [Preventing and averting illness] is an 1867 Chinese medical
  and everyday-health booklet by Frederick Porter Smith. Bodleian Libraries, Sinica
  2044, is catalogued as published in Hankow (漢皋); its title page names Baoshan jushe
  (寶善居舍) as block holder. It discusses housing, diet, mental and physical activity,
  cleanliness, internal and external medicine, eye conditions, women’s health and
  infant care, linking health discourse with Christian vocabulary. It supports historical
  work on medical translation, missionary publishing and everyday life in late-Qing
  China. Based on 21 digital captures, TXT, JSON and TEI XML provide the transcription
  of 36 designated physical faces, uncertainties and source-image mappings. Following
  the AI initial transcription, this version applies a separate AI source-only reading
  of all 36 faces, full comparison and nonblind source-image adjudication. Full human
  double collation and expert review have not been performed; unresolved readings
  and clipped marginal material remain marked.
